verification_notes:
EmitCIEBasedDEF dereferences `Pipeline->Elements` into local `mpe` and immediately passes it to `cmsStageInputChannels(mpe)` and other helpers without any NULL check.  From the only direct caller WriteInputLUT:

```
DeviceLink = cmsPipelineDup(v->Lut);
if (DeviceLink == NULL) return FALSE;   // only pointer checked
...
_cmsOptimizePipeline(..., &DeviceLink, ...);
// _cmsOptimizePipeline may remove every stage and leaves (*PtrLut)->Elements == NULL
rc = EmitCIEBasedDEF(m, DeviceLink, Intent, &BlackPointAdaptedToD50);
```

Inside `_cmsOptimizePipeline`, when the last stage is removed, it explicitly leaves `Elements == NULL` and just sets evaluation parameters:

```
if ((*PtrLut)->Elements == NULL) {
    _cmsPipelineSetOptimizationParameters(...);
    return TRUE;
}
```

Therefore `DeviceLink` can be non-NULL while `DeviceLink->Elements` is NULL, leading to a NULL-pointer dereference exactly at the suspicious line when EmitCIEBasedDEF tries to access it.

Input reachability: the path `cms_postscript_fuzzer → cmsGetPostScriptCSA/CRD → WriteInputLUT → EmitCIEBasedDEF` is present in the static call graph.  The pipeline contents originate from the attacker-controlled ICC profile opened from the fuzzer input, so the condition that empties the element list depends on that input.

No guard re-checks `Pipeline->Elements` before the access, so no suppression upstream is present.

ASan would raise an invalid memory access on this NULL dereference, so the bug class is observable by the configured AddressSanitizer.
